Contact material callback: the user material ids of both surfaces

b3ContactMaterial grows two inputs at its end, userMaterialIdA and userMaterialIdB: the
b3SurfaceMaterial::userMaterialId of the surface each shape touches at this contact, in the order
of the callback's shapeIdA and shapeIdB. A write to them is ignored. No existing field moves, so a
caller that never reads them is unchanged.

A convex contact, and a compound child contact against a convex shape, take each shape's material;
the pairs the narrow phase flips are swapped back to the contact's order. A mesh or height-field
contact takes for shape A the material under most of its manifold points (the lowest index on a
tie) and for shape B its material. The ids are stored on the contact when its manifold is updated,
and only while a contact material callback is set, so a world without one does no extra work (each
contact does grow by two 64-bit fields); setting
the callback already makes every contact update once.

